1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,034
Smart contracts run for years, and there's no tail -f for a live protocol.

2
00:00:05,034 --> 00:00:10,953
When something breaks, you hear about it on Telegram, an hour after the funds are gone.

3
00:00:10,953 --> 00:00:15,988
Argus turns Splunk into a security operations center for cross-chain DeFi.

4
00:00:15,988 --> 00:00:19,790
This is Argus monitoring LayerZero, live in Splunk.

5
00:00:19,790 --> 00:00:26,501
Every transaction, event, source file, and audit report becomes a typed Splunk sourcetype.

6
00:00:26,501 --> 00:00:31,496
Hundreds of thousands of transactions indexed across the in-scope LayerZero contracts.

7
00:00:31,496 --> 00:00:37,410
Detection is pure SPL: per-contract z-score baselines that auto-tune, with conservative floors to suppress dust.

8
00:00:37,410 --> 00:00:40,594
The contract teaches Splunk what's normal.

9
00:00:40,594 --> 00:00:46,811
And Argus is honest: it separates real anomalies from ordinary protocol lifecycle.

10
00:00:46,811 --> 00:00:53,180
We disabled a rule that flagged normal LayerZero message delivery as replay attacks.

11
00:00:53,180 --> 00:00:55,834
A clean baseline beats crying wolf.

12
00:00:55,834 --> 00:01:01,322
An AI agent runs inside the Splunk app itself, a modular input on the Python SDK.

13
00:01:01,322 --> 00:01:06,810
It triages each finding in-process and writes its verdict back as a Splunk event.

14
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:12,569
Here it surfaced nine value-manipulation candidates, led by Puffer pufETH.

15
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:16,702
And the detections themselves are written by Splunk's own AI:

16
00:01:16,702 --> 00:01:22,868
I describe a threat in plain English, and the Splunk AI Assistant writes the SPL detection,

17
00:01:22,868 --> 00:01:27,950
in about fifteen seconds. The AI builds the security logic; Splunk runs it.

18
00:01:27,950 --> 00:01:31,530
High-severity candidates become proof-of-concept triggers.

19
00:01:31,530 --> 00:01:35,172
An external Anvil mainnet fork runs a Foundry exploit test,

20
00:01:35,172 --> 00:01:40,975
and Argus marks a finding confirmed only when the test's own assertions reproduce the exploit.

21
00:01:40,975 --> 00:01:44,246
Never on a guess, and never with a fabricated number.

22
00:01:44,246 --> 00:01:50,496
Everything runs on Splunk: Splunk does the pattern work, holds the state, runs the agent,

23
00:01:50,496 --> 00:01:56,184
and even writes its own detections with Splunk's hosted AI. The production verdict path is Splunk-native.

24
00:01:56,184 --> 00:01:59,555
Argus is what using Splunk correctly looks like.

25
00:01:59,555 --> 00:02:03,487
Open source, built for the Splunk Agentic Ops Hackathon.
